Relevant tables & columns for the task  
• legislators_terms.id_bioguide : primary key that links the two tables  
• legislators_terms.state : two–letter state abbreviation we must finally return  
• legislators_terms.term_start / term_end : determine when somebody is “in office”  
• legislators_terms.term_type : we might restrict to Reps/Senators if desired (not strictly required)  
• legislators.gender : split the cohort into M and F  
• legislators.id_bioguide : join key to legislators_terms  
Conditions we will probably need  
• Join legislators_terms → legislators on id_bioguide to get gender and state.  
• Convert term_start / term_end (text ‘YYYY‑MM‑DD’) to dates with SQLite’s date()/strftime().  
• For each legislator take the MIN(term_start) = “first term”.  
• Build six “evaluation dates” – Dec‑31 of start‑year + 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10.  
• A legislator is “retained” at an interval if any of his/her terms encloses the evaluation date (eval_date BETWEEN term_start AND term_end).  
• Compute retention‑rate by state & gender; finally keep only those states where BOTH genders have a non‑zero rate at ALL six checkpoints.

Below are ten exploratory → building‑block → final SQL queries (all SQLite‐compatible).  
Each query is preceded by a short comment, returns ≤ 20 rows with DISTINCT wherever useful, and only uses the provided tables.

```sql
-- 1) Peek at the distinct state abbreviations that appear in legislators_terms
SELECT DISTINCT "state"
FROM "legislators_terms"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Show a few legislators together with their gender
SELECT "id_bioguide", "full_name", "gender"
FROM "legislators"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Join the two tables to inspect state, gender, term_start / term_end
SELECT lt."id_bioguide",
       l."gender",
       lt."state",
       lt."term_start",
       lt."term_end"
FROM   "legislators_terms" AS lt
JOIN   "legislators"       AS l
  ON   lt."id_bioguide" = l."id_bioguide"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) For every legislator get the first‑term start date (earliest term_start)
SELECT l."id_bioguide",
       l."gender",
       lt."state",
       MIN(lt."term_start") AS "first_term_start"
FROM   "legislators_terms" AS lt
JOIN   "legislators"       AS l
  ON   lt."id_bioguide" = l."id_bioguide"
GROUP  BY l."id_bioguide"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Check whether a legislator is still in office on Dec‑31 of his/her first term year (offset 0)
SELECT b."id_bioguide",
       b."state",
       b."gender",
       CASE
         WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1
                      FROM   "legislators_terms" AS t
                      WHERE  t."id_bioguide" = b."id_bioguide"
                        AND  DATE( CAST(STRFTIME('%Y', b."first_term_start") AS INTEGER) || '-12-31')
                             BETWEEN t."term_start" AND t."term_end" )
         THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS "retained_yr0"
FROM (
      -- subquery is the result of query 4 (first_term_start)
      SELECT l."id_bioguide",
             l."gender",
             lt."state",
             MIN(lt."term_start") AS "first_term_start"
      FROM   "legislators_terms" AS lt
      JOIN   "legislators"       AS l
        ON   lt."id_bioguide" = l."id_bioguide"
      GROUP  BY l."id_bioguide"
     ) AS b
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Same idea but test the +2 year checkpoint (Dec‑31 two years after first term start)
SELECT b."id_bioguide",
       b."state",
       b."gender",
       CASE
         WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1
                      FROM   "legislators_terms" AS t
                      WHERE  t."id_bioguide" = b."id_bioguide"
                        AND  DATE( (CAST(STRFTIME('%Y', b."first_term_start") AS INTEGER)+2) || '-12-31')
                             BETWEEN t."term_start" AND t."term_end" )
         THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS "retained_yr2"
FROM (
      SELECT l."id_bioguide",
             l."gender",
             lt."state",
             MIN(lt."term_start") AS "first_term_start"
      FROM   "legislators_terms" AS lt
      JOIN   "legislators"       AS l
        ON   lt."id_bioguide" = l."id_bioguide"
      GROUP  BY l."id_bioguide"
     ) AS b
LIMIT 20;
